      en: "Hegmatana Hill in Hamadan, Iran, is the archaeological core of ancient Ecbatana, the summer capital of the Medes. The site contains stratified remains from the Median, Achaemenid, Parthian, and Sassanid periods. Excavations have uncovered massive stone walls and palace foundations, indicating advanced urban planning. The hill covers approximately 40 hectares and rises 20 meters above the surrounding plain. The Greek historian Herodotus described Ecbatana as a city with seven concentric walls of different colors. The site has been a protected Iranian national heritage site since 1931 and was added to the UNESCO tentative list in 2024.",
