      en: "Alexander's Prison (Zendān-e Eskandar) in Yazd is a 12th-century building originally built as a madrasa (theological school). Local legend associates it with Alexander the Great, claiming he imprisoned his captives here, though there is no historical evidence. The structure features an octagonal room topped by a high dome decorated with stucco and tilework. A courtyard with a pool surrounds the building. Today, it serves as a museum showcasing Yazd's history and folklore. The architecture is typical of the Ilkhanid period, with intricate muqarnas and brick patterns.",
